# The preferred citation is the paper that describes PlainPairs, on arXiv. To name the exact
# data used, cite the dataset's version on Hugging Face as well (under references). Zenodo
# archives each GitHub release of this repository from .zenodo.json (which it reads in place of
# this file); the DOI below stands for all versions of the software and documents and resolves
# to the latest.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use PlainPairs, please cite the paper below. To name the exact data you used, also cite the dataset and its version (under references)."
type: software
title: "PlainPairs: a research corpus pairing the prescriber and patient texts of US medicine labels"
abstract: >-
  PlainPairs is a research corpus of how United States medicine labels are worded for two
  readers: for every medicine whose label carries a version written for patients (a Medication
  Guide or a patient package insert), the text written for prescribers next to the text written
  for patients, matched by section, with reading-level and plain-language measures on both
  sides. It is a record of wording, not medical information.
